\chapter{Memoization and Lazy Values}
\label{ch:memoization-and-lazy}

\section{Two Deferrals, Two Purposes}

A DSL that evaluates rules, parses grammars, or computes normal forms tends
to contain functions whose results are expensive to produce and cheap to
re-use. The classic example is a rewrite rule set that is applied at every
node of a tree: the rules themselves do not change, but the interpreter that
applies them is asked the same questions over and over. Two different
mechanisms address this, and \DSLtk{} ships both.

\emph{Memoisation} answers the question ``have I computed this before?'' by
keying a cache on the argument. \code{dsl::memoize} wraps a function of one
argument in a cache, so that each distinct argument is computed once and
every later call with the same argument returns the stored answer.

\emph{Laziness} answers the question ``does anyone want this yet?'' by
postponing a computation until it is first demanded. \code{dsl::Lazy} wraps a
producer in a one-shot cache: the producer runs at most once in the entire
lifetime of the object, and only if someone asks.

The two are easy to confuse because both are implemented with a cache. They
differ in what the cache is keyed on, how many values it can hold, and what
the caller receives. Section~\ref{sec:ch19-compare} sets them side by side.

\section{A Worked Example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch19-memo} is a complete program that exercises both. It
counts how many times the underlying functions actually run, which is the
only honest way to demonstrate that a cache is doing its job.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Memoisation and laziness side by side. The evaluation counters show that the memoised square runs its body once for two calls with the same key, that the memoised Fibonacci computes each index once, and that the lazy producer runs exactly once however many times it is read.},label={lst:ch19-memo}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>

int main ()
{
  std::cout << std::boolalpha;

  // ---------------- memoize ----------------
  long long square_evaluations = 0;
  auto square = dsl::memoize<int, long long> (
      [&square_evaluations] (int x) -> long long {
        ++square_evaluations;
        return static_cast<long long> (x) * x;
      });

  std::cout << "square(12)     = " << square (12)
            << "  evaluations: " << square_evaluations << "\n";
  std::cout << "square(12)     = " << square (12)
            << "  evaluations: " << square_evaluations << "\n";

  square.clear_cache ();
  square_evaluations = 0;
  square (12);
  std::cout << "after clear_cache, evaluations: " << square_evaluations << "\n";

  // A recursive function whose memoized wrapper calls back into itself.
  long long fib_evaluations = 0;
  std::function<long long (int)> fib;
  fib = dsl::memoize<int, long long> (
      [&fib, &fib_evaluations] (int n) -> long long {
        ++fib_evaluations;
        return n < 2 ? n : fib (n - 1) + fib (n - 2);
      });

  std::cout << "fib(40)        = " << fib (40)
            << "  evaluations: " << fib_evaluations << "\n";
  fib_evaluations = 0;
  std::cout << "fib(40) again  = " << fib (40)
            << "  evaluations: " << fib_evaluations << "\n";

  // ---------------- Lazy ----------------
  int builds = 0;
  dsl::Lazy<std::string> configuration ([&builds] {
    ++builds;
    return std::string ("expensive configuration");
  });

  std::cout << "is_computed before get(): " << configuration.is_computed () << "\n";
  std::cout << "get()  -> " << configuration.get () << "\n";
  std::cout << "is_computed after  get(): " << configuration.is_computed () << "\n";
  std::cout << "producer invocations: " << builds << "\n";
  std::cout << "force() equals get(): " << (configuration.force () == configuration.get ())
            << "\n";

  // ---------------- the Memoization mixin ----------------
  struct CountingDSL : dsl::DSL<CountingDSL, dsl::Memoization>
  {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> memo_cache;
  };

  CountingDSL d;
  d.memo_cache["x"] = 1;
  d.memo_cache["y"] = 2;
  std::cout << "memo_cache size before clear: " << d.memo_cache.size () << "\n";
  d.clear_cache ();
  std::cout << "memo_cache size after  clear: " << d.memo_cache.size () << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch19-memo}. The Fibonacci result is computed by forty-one calls rather than the hundreds of millions an unmemoised recursion would need; the second call to the same index costs nothing at all.},label={lst:ch19-memo-out}]
square(12)     = 144  evaluations: 1
square(12)     = 144  evaluations: 1
after clear_cache, evaluations: 1
fib(40)        = 102334155  evaluations: 41
fib(40) again  = 102334155  evaluations: 0
is_computed before get(): false
get()  -> expensive configuration
is_computed after  get(): true
producer invocations: 1
force() equals get(): true
memo_cache size before clear: 2
memo_cache size after  clear: 0
\end{lstlisting}

The Fibonacci case is the one worth pausing over. The lambda passed to
\code{memoize} calls \code{fib}, the \code{std::function} that the memoised
wrapper was assigned to, so the recursive calls go through the cache as well.
Without the cache the same computation would need a number of calls
exponential in the argument; with it, exactly forty-one, one for each index
from zero to forty. The trick of capturing the wrapper by reference through a
\code{std::function} is the standard way to write a self-recursive memoised
function in \cpp{}, and the header imposes no obstacle to it.

\section{The Data Model}
\label{sec:ch19-compare}

\code{dsl::MemoizedCallable<Key, Value, Fn>} (lines 7356--7381) is the
memoising wrapper. It has two private members: the wrapped callable
\code{Fn fn_} and a cache, which is a \code{std::unordered_map<Key, Value>}.
The constructor is \code{explicit MemoizedCallable(Fn fn)}.

\code{dsl::memoize<Key, Value>(fn)} (lines 7382--7388) is the factory. It
takes the callable by forwarding reference, decays it, and returns
\code{MemoizedCallable<Key, Value, std::decay_t<Fn>>}.

\code{dsl::Lazy<T>} (lines 7404--7436) is the lazy cell. It has two private
members: a \code{std::function<T ()> producer_} and a
\code{std::optional<T> value_}. The empty \code{optional} is the "not yet
computed" flag; the presence of a value in it is the flag. The constructor
is \code{explicit Lazy(std::function<T ()> producer)}.

\code{dsl::Memoization} (lines 7290--7301) is a feature tag of a different
kind. Its mixin adds a single member function, \code{clear_cache()}, whose
body is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The \code{Memoization} feature mixin, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 7290--7301. It reaches into the deriving type for a member called \code{memo_cache} and empties it.},label={lst:ch19-mixin}]
struct Memoization
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// Clears all cached entries stored in `Derived::memo_cache`.
    void
    clear_cache ()
    {
      static_cast<Derived &> (*this).memo_cache.clear ();
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

This is worth pausing on, because it is easy to mistake for the cache
inside \code{MemoizedCallable}. It is not. The mixin does not create a
cache; it empties a map that the \emph{user} provides as a member of the
deriving type, conventionally named \code{memo_cache}. The header's comment
says as much: the deriving DSL is expected to define \code{memo_cache},
typically an unordered map. There is no \code{requires} clause, so a DSL
that names the feature without providing the member fails to compile with an
error about a missing member rather than a diagnosable constraint violation.

The two caches are entirely separate. \code{MemoizedCallable} has a private
cache it owns and will never expose; the mixin has a cache the user owns and
fills by hand. A DSL that wants a memoisation facility it can inspect and
invalidate should use the mixin and write the lookups itself; a DSL that
wants a function memoised automatically should use \code{memoize}.

