\section{The Memoised Call}

The call sequence of \code{MemoizedCallable::operator()} is three steps,
and Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch19-memo} states it. The \code{find} is followed by
a computation and then an \code{emplace}; the \code{emplace} is a no-op if
the key is already present, but it cannot be, because the \code{find} just
established that it was not.

\begin{pseudocode}[A memoised call]
\textbf{function} call (key)\\
\quad \textbf{if} the cache contains key then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} a copy of the cached value\\
\quad value := the wrapped function (key)\\
\quad insert value into the cache under key\\
\quad \textbf{return} a copy of value
\label{alg:ch19-memo}
\end{pseudocode}

Four consequences follow from the code as written, and all four are
observable.

The operator is \emph{non-const}. The declaration is
\code{Value operator() (const Key &key)}, with no trailing \code{const},
because it mutates the cache. A \code{const} memoised callable cannot be
called at all. This is a genuine constraint on how the wrapper can be
stored: it cannot be a \code{const} member of a class whose methods are
\code{const}, and it cannot be placed in a \code{std::optional} that is
inspected through a \code{const} reference.

The value is returned \emph{by copy}. The two return statements are
\code{return it->second;} and \code{return value;}, both of which copy the
stored object. Nothing is returned by reference or by move. A cached value
of a type that is expensive to copy is copied on every call, which is a real
cost that the cache does not avoid.

The key type must be usable with \code{std::hash}. The cache is a plain
\code{std::unordered_map<Key, Value>}, so the standard hash and the
standard equality are used, and there is no mechanism for supplying
alternatives. A key type that needs a bespoke hash has to provide a
specialisation of \code{std::hash}, or be replaced by a type that does.

The header says plainly that the wrapper is "intentionally not thread-safe"
(line 7344). Two threads calling the same memoised callable concurrently
may both miss the cache, both run the function, and both attempt to
\code{emplace}; the \code{unordered_map} is not safe for concurrent
mutation, and the result is a data race rather than merely a duplicated
computation.

