\section{The Lazy Cell}

\code{Lazy<T>} is the classic "thunk with a flag", written once and
generically. The pattern it implements is old: a value that is not yet
computed, a flag saying whether it has been, and a producer that fills it
in on demand. Written by hand for a specific type it would look like this:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The thunk-with-a-flag pattern that \code{dsl::Lazy} generalises. The sketch assumes a user-supplied \code{load_disk} function. The hand-written version names one value type; the header's version uses \code{std::optional<T>} for the flag and the value together, and \code{std::function<T ()>} for the producer.},label={lst:ch19-thunk}]
struct Config
{
  bool computed = false;
  std::string value;

  std::string const & get ()
  {
    if (!computed)
      {
        value = load_from_disk ();   // expensive
        computed = true;
      }
    return value;
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

\code{Lazy} is that pattern with the value type and the producer turned into
template parameters. The \code{std::optional<T>} member plays both roles at
once: an empty optional is the flag, and a populated optional is the value.
This is a neat economy, and it has one consequence worth noting: because
the flag and the value are the same object, there is no way to have a
computed value that is itself empty or default-constructed. A
\code{Lazy<std::optional<int>>} can hold an empty optional inside a
populated optional, which is fine, but a \code{Lazy<int>} that computes the
value zero is indistinguishable from one that has not been computed only by
inspecting \code{is_computed()}.

The public surface is three members.

\code{get()} evaluates the producer if it has not already run, and returns a
\emph{reference} to the cached value. The return type is \code{T&}, not
\code{T}, so a caller that wants to mutate the value in place can, and a
caller that binds a reference does not pay for a copy.

\code{force()} is an alias for \code{get()}. The two names exist because
``force'' reads better at a call site that is explicitly demanding a value
and ``get'' reads better when it is not obvious. There is no behavioural
difference whatsoever; \code{force()} is a one-line forward.

\code{is_computed()} is \code{const noexcept} and reports whether the
producer has fired. It is the only way to ask the question without forcing
the value.

What is \emph{absent} is as important as what is present. There is no
\code{value()}, no \code{operator()}, no \code{reset()}, and no
\code{const} overload of \code{get()}. In particular:

\begin{itemize}
\item Because \code{get()} is non-const, a \code{const Lazy<T>} cannot be
read at all. A lazy cell in a \code{const} context, or in a \code{const}
member function, is unusable.
\item Because there is no \code{reset()}, a forced value is permanent. The
producer cannot be re-run and the value cannot be discarded.
\item Because there is no \code{value()} and no \code{operator()}, a
\code{Lazy<T>} does not behave like a value at all. It must be explicitly
forced at every point of use, which is arguably the correct design: the
whole point is that reading a lazy cell is a deliberate act.
\item Because \code{get()} returns a reference, the reference dangles if
the \code{Lazy} is destroyed, and it dangles if the \code{Lazy} is moved,
because the move relocates the \code{std::optional} and with it the value
that the reference pointed at. Storing \code{const T&} obtained from
\code{get()} is safe only as long as the cell itself outlives it.
\end{itemize}

The header is explicit that the type is "not thread-safe by default" (line
7396). Two threads calling \code{get()} on the same cell may both observe
an empty optional and both run the producer, and the assignment to the
optional is a race.

\subsection{Using \code{Lazy} to Defer an AST Construction}

The typical use of \code{Lazy} in a DSL is to defer the construction of an
expensive syntax tree until some operation actually needs it. A grammar that
is read from a file, or a rule set that is compiled from a table, can be
built on first use and never rebuilt. Listing~\ref{lst:ch19-deferred} is a
fragment that does exactly this.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Deferring the construction of a rule list until a rule is actually needed. The producer is a captureless lambda converted to the \code{std::function<T ()>} that the \code{Lazy} constructor takes; the list is built once, on the first \code{get()}.},label={lst:ch19-deferred}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using Rule = dsl::ASTNode;

// The rules are not built until somebody asks for them.
dsl::Lazy<std::vector<Rule>> rules ([] {
  std::vector<Rule> v;
  v.push_back (dsl::node<"rule"> (dsl::leaf<"name"> ("x"),
                                  dsl::leaf<"body"> ("y")));
  return v;
});

int main ()
{
  std::cout << rules.get ().front ().dump () << "\n";   // (rule (name x) (body y))
}
\end{lstlisting}

The advantage over building the vector eagerly is not merely that the work
is skipped when the rules are never consulted. It is that the cost moves
out of the start-up path and into the first use, which is often where it
belongs: a program that only ever prints help text never pays for the
grammar at all.

\section{Eager and Lazy Evaluation}

The distinction the two facilities embody is the distinction between
\emph{eager} and \emph{lazy} evaluation, and it is worth stating the trade
precisely rather than in the usual slogans.

An eager computation is performed when the expression is written down. The
value exists immediately, errors in it surface immediately, and the cost is
paid whether or not the value is used. A lazy computation is performed when
the value is demanded. Errors in it surface at the point of demand, which
may be much later and in a different part of the program; the cost is paid
only if the value is actually needed.

Lazy evaluation buys three things. It avoids work that is never needed. It
permits data definitions that are circular in the eager world -- a tree
node that refers to its parent, a grammar that refers to itself -- because
the reference is only resolved when it is followed. And it permits a value
to be shared, since the cached reference can be handed out repeatedly
without copying.

It costs three things as well. The "forced" state is sticky, so a lazy cell
cannot be reset and a memoised cache cannot be shrunk except by clearing it
outright. The point at which an error is reported moves from the point of
definition to the point of use, which is a genuine debugging cost: a
misbehaving producer throws from a place that has nothing to do with the
code that installed it. And the discipline of forcing must be imposed by the
programmer, because nothing in the type system prevents a lazy cell from
being ignored entirely, in which case its producer never runs and its
effects never happen.

The \code{Lazy} type is a very small expression of this trade. It has no
notion of laziness beyond the single flag, and it makes no attempt to be a
general thunk language: there is no \code{map}, no \code{flat_map}, no
composition of thunks. It is a single-shot cell, and it is honest about
being one.

\section{Choosing Between the Three Facilities}

There are three ways to avoid repeating work in this toolkit, and they are
not interchangeable.

\code{dsl::memoize} is for a function whose result depends on a single
hashable argument. It is the right tool when the argument space is small
and the computation is expensive: a normalisation of a token, a
canonicalisation of an identifier, a hash of a subtree. The programmer
writes the function once and gets caching for free, at the cost of having
to name the key and value types explicitly.

\code{dsl::Lazy<T>} is for a computation with no argument at all, or with
an argument that has been captured rather than passed. It is the right tool
when a value is expensive to produce but is usually not needed at all: a
grammar read from a file, a configuration assembled from the environment, a
rule set compiled from a table. The single-shot semantics are exactly
right, because the thing being deferred has no notion of a key.

The \code{Memoization} mixin is for neither of those, and it is the option
a DSL author is most likely to need. The mixin does not wrap a callable;
it gives the deriving type a method that empties a map the type owns. The
DSL author then writes the lookups explicitly, which buys three things
that \code{memoize} cannot offer. The cache may be keyed on more than one
thing, for instance on a rule name and a node kind together. The cache may
be inspected: the DSL can report how many entries it holds, or seed it
from a table at start-up. And the cache may be invalidated selectively, by
removing the entries for one key, rather than wholesale.

The price is that the lookups are written by hand, and a hand-written
lookup can be wrong in ways a wrapper cannot. The canonical form is short
enough to state: look the key up; if it is present, use the stored value;
otherwise compute it, store it, and use it. Written that way it is exactly
the body of \code{MemoizedCallable::operator()}, and a DSL that gets it
right behaves identically to a wrapped function. Written carelessly -- by
forgetting to store the computed value, or by storing it under a
differently-spelled key -- it will recompute silently, and the symptom will
be a slowdown rather than a failure. That is why the mixin's documentation
is careful to say only that it empties the map, and says nothing about how
the map is filled.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{\code{memoize} does not deduce its template arguments.} Both
\code{Key} and \code{Value} must be written: \code{dsl::memoize<int, long
long>(fn)}. Neither can be inferred, because the function's parameter and
return types are hidden inside a generic lambda, and the header does not
attempt to inspect them. Writing \code{dsl::memoize(fn)} is a compile error
with no candidate template.

\item \textbf{\code{memoize} is not \code{constexpr}, and neither is
\code{Lazy}.} Neither factory is declared \code{constexpr}, and neither
class has a \code{constexpr} constructor. A cached computation cannot
therefore participate in a constant expression, which rules out using
\code{memoize} for compile-time tables.

\item \textbf{The cache has no custom hash or equality.} The map is a plain
\code{std::unordered_map<Key, Value>}. A key type that needs a custom
hash must specialise \code{std::hash} for it; there is no template
parameter for it.

\item \textbf{Cached values are copied on every call.} Both return
statements in \code{operator()} copy. A \code{Value} that is expensive to
copy makes the cache less useful than it looks.

\item \textbf{The wrapper is copyable, and copying it copies the cache.}
\code{MemoizedCallable} has no user-declared copy operations, so the
implicit ones apply and the \code{unordered_map} is copied along with the
callable. Copying a memoised callable therefore copies its accumulated
results, which is occasionally surprising.

\item \textbf{There is no thread safety, and the header says so.} Neither
facility uses a mutex, an atomic, or any other synchronisation. The
\code{unordered_map} is not safe for concurrent mutation, and the
\code{std::optional} flag in \code{Lazy} is not safe for concurrent
check-then-set. A concurrent caller may also observe a torn value.

\item \textbf{\code{Lazy} stores the producer as a \code{std::function}.}
That means the producer is type-erased and must be copy-constructible, and
that a \code{Lazy} capturing a large object by value copies that object
into the \code{std::function}. The D port does not have this restriction:
its \code{Lazy} is parameterised on the producer type as well as the value
type, and it provides a \code{make_lazy} factory that infers the value type
from the producer. The \cpp{} port has no \code{make_lazy}; the value type
must be spelled out.

\item \textbf{A forced \code{Lazy} cannot be unforced.} There is no
\code{reset()} and no way to replace the producer. A \code{Lazy} that has
been forced behaves like a value that happens to have been computed
recently.

\item \textbf{The reference returned by \code{get()} can dangle.} It
dangles if the cell is destroyed, and it dangles if the cell is moved,
because the move relocates the cached value.

\item \textbf{The \code{Memoization} mixin requires a user-provided
member.} It calls \code{memo_cache.clear()} on a member of the deriving
type, with no \code{requires} clause and no default. A DSL that names the
feature and does not supply the member does not compile.

\item \textbf{The two caches do not interact.} \code{MemoizedCallable}'s
cache is private and cannot be cleared from outside except through the
wrapper's own \code{clear_cache()}; the mixin's cache is the user's and is
never touched by \code{memoize}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The two facilities in this chapter are deliberately small. \code{memoize}
is a hash map with a wrapper around it, and \code{Lazy} is an optional
value with a flag. Neither is clever, and neither tries to be; what they
provide is a standard, auditable place to put a cache, in a library whose
other facilities are all about making the structure of a computation
visible. The memoised Fibonacci in Listing~\ref{lst:ch19-memo} computes
its forty-first distinct input exactly once, and the lazy configuration in
the same listing is built exactly once, or not at all.

The next chapter changes subject from \emph{when} a computation happens to
\emph{whether} it succeeds. \code{dsl::Maybe} and \code{dsl::Result} give
the library a way to represent the absence of a value and the failure of a
step, and to propagate that failure through a chain of transformations
without exceptions.
